## Interior Mutability with Cell

`Cell[T]` provides interior mutability — you can mutate the inner value through an immutable reference. It works with Copy types.

### The Problem Cell Solves

```xiom
// Normal immutable reference — can't mutate
let x = 42;
// Cannot change x through &x

// Cell allows mutation through shared references
let cell = Cell.new(42);
cell.set(100);  // mutate through immutable reference
let val = cell.get();  // 100
```

### Cell Operations

```xiom
let c = Cell.new(0);

c.set(42);          // replace the value
let v = c.get();    // get the value
c.set(v + 1);       // increment
```

### Cell with Rc

```xiom
let shared = Rc.new(Cell.new(0));

// Multiple owners can all mutate
let clone = shared.clone();
shared.set(10);
clone.set(20);  // both see the same cell
// shared.get() == 20
```

### Why Cell?

Without Cell, you can't mutate data behind an immutable reference. Cell is safe because:
- It only works with Copy types (no borrowing issues)
- It doesn't give out references (get returns a copy, not a reference)
- No aliasing problems possible

### Cell Limitations

- Only works with Copy types
- No references into the cell (get always copies)
- For non-Copy types, use RefCell

### Common Patterns

```xiom
// Counter shared across owners
let counter = Rc.new(Cell.new(0));
// Track state without &mut
let state = Cell.new(Status.Idle);
state.set(Status.Running);
```

